  "summary": "Darkening of image corners or edges, either as an optical artifact or intentional aesthetic effect.",
  "description": "Characterized by gradual darkening from center to edges of an image; caused by lens design limitations or intentionally added in post-processing. Originally an optical limitation in early photography, later became a deliberate aesthetic choice in fine art and commercial photography to draw attention to the center.",
  "culturalContext": "Heavy vignetting — the edge-darkening of cheap lenses, wide apertures and toy cameras (Holga, Lomography culture) — flipped from defect to aesthetic: it focuses the eye, fakes intimacy and period, and its \"lens-baby\" and film-emulation filters are now standard. Renaissance painting already used the device (dark-edge portraits); photographers from Julia Margaret Cameron through Instagram's VSCO era made the darkened frame the grammar of subjective memory.",
